<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philo of Alexandria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 20 BCE–c. 50 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> was a Jewish philosopher who lived in Alexandria and wrote in Greek. He interpreted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> scriptures allegorically in the light of Greek, especially Platonic, philosophy, and his works influenced early Christian theologians. Around 40 CE he led an embassy of Alexandrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> to the emperor Gaius (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a>) after violent riots in the city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philo-of-alexandria.jpg" alt="Philo of Alexandria" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: André Thévet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PhiloThevet.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
